# @public — sadish stroker: path -> stroke coverage, round (0.4.0) or styled caps + joins.
#
# stroke.cyr — two entry points over one flatten step:
#   sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width)                                  round caps, round joins
#   sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit)       SD_CAP_* x SD_JOIN_*
# Both flatten curve verbs at tol = SD_ONE >> 2 into the stroker's run (the _sd_stroke_* scratch in
# raster.cyr) and MAX-combine the stroke into the canvas coverage, so it unions with whatever the
# canvas already holds and honours the clip.
# ⭐ A THIRD, sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash (src/dash.cyr), is the STYLED path with its run cut into
# dashes. It shares _sd_styled_walk below verb for verb and differs only in what a subpath is handed
# to: _sd_sb_subpath whole, or _sd_dash_subpath piece by piece. So a dash is drawn by exactly the
# code below, into exactly the same batch — the only arrow out of this file is _sd_sb_dispatch's.
#
# ⛔ ROUND/ROUND IS THE 0.4.0 STROKER, UNCHANGED. `_ex(cv, p, w, SD_CAP_ROUND, SD_JOIN_ROUND, any)`
# returns `sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, p, w)`, whose code moved here from raster.cyr verbatim: a
# rect per flattened segment, then a 16-gon disc per run point (duplicates, collinear points and
# the closing vertex included), each its OWN sd_canvas_fill_union pass. Byte-identical to 0.5.5,
# asserted against a verbatim copy in programs/stroke_style_test.cyr. ⚠ So ROUND/ROUND alone keeps
# three 0.4.0 behaviours no other cap/join pair has: a drawing verb after CLOSE continues the same
# run ("M 4,4 L 28,4 L 28,20 Z L 4,20" strokes a closed rectangle; the styled path, like the fill,
# reads a triangle and then (4,4) -> (4,20)); a pixel two pieces split reads the MAX of their
# parts, not their sum (below); and a path per piece on the seam — 3,232 B per closed-rect stroke
# since 0.8.0 opened each at its exact capacity, 34,432 B for the same 104 allocations before that.
#
# ⭐ THE STYLED PATH COLLECTS ITS PIECES AS ONE EDGE LIST AND FILLS IT ONCE. Piece-per-pass MAX
# union is not a union: where two pieces share an edge INSIDE a pixel, the pixel gets max(a, b),
# not a + b. MEASURED: two 3-px-tall rects meeting at x = 4.5, fill_union'd separately, leave pixel
# 4 at 127; as one nonzero fill, 255. The round stroker hides that seam under the disc it puts on
# every vertex; a BUTT/MITER/BEVEL stroke has nothing to hide it. So the pieces (segment rects, join
# triangles, discs, square caps) go into a process-lifetime edge batch, every one wound the SAME
# way (negative shoelace, like the segment rect) — winding adds, overlap is exact, and a piece of
# the opposite winding would punch a hole, which is why each triangle's order is decided from its
# own signed area rather than reasoned about.
# ⭐ AND IT FILLS THAT BATCH WITH ITS OWN WALK (_sd_sb_flush), NOT sd_canvas_fill_union. Same
# sampling as the default fill — SD_FILL_SS sub-scanlines, analytic x, the same crossing formula —
# but a run of overlapping or touching pieces is ONE span. sd_fill_impl's nonzero walk adds a span
# per crossing and floors each, so a solid pixel that a piece edge crosses reads 254. MEASURED, a
# width-8 cubic filled through fill_union: 120 such pixels under MITER, 282 under ROUND joins; a
# straight polyline with 10 fractional vertices at width 4: 36. Through the merged walk: 2, 2 and 0
# — the cubic's two sit on the band's rim, where the 0.4.0 round stroker reads 254 too.
# ⚠ The default fill is not changed for this: merging spans there would move its bytes for
# self-overlapping nonzero paths. ⚠ And because the flush does not go through sd_fill_impl, a
# coverage choice made there does not reach styled strokes unless the flush is taught it — so it
# IS taught the one that exists: on an SD_AA_AREA canvas the batch goes to _sd_area_fill
# (src/coverage.cyr) as a nonzero MAX-combine, where touching pieces add exactly and no bridge is
# needed. Same guard as sd_fill_impl: only a batch that dropped no edge (_sd_sb_trunc == 0).
#
# SEMANTICS (styled path).
#   · Consecutive coincident points are dropped first; a closed subpath whose last point equals
#     its moveto point loses that duplicate, so a zero-length closing segment makes no join.
#   · Open subpath: a cap at each end, a join at every interior vertex. Closed (SD_VERB_CLOSE): no
#     caps, a join at EVERY vertex, the start/end vertex included. A drawing verb after a CLOSE
#     starts a new subpath at the moveto point, as the fill reads it.
#   · A subpath with no non-zero-length segment (a lone moveto, or every point coincident, closed
#     or not): ROUND -> a disc of radius hw, SQUARE -> an axis-aligned square of side 2*hw centred
#     on the point, BUTT -> nothing. This is the common renderer behaviour for "M x y Z" (strict
#     SVG draws nothing for a lone moveto).
#   · BUTT: the segment rect only. SQUARE: a rect extending the segment by hw past its end.
#     ROUND: the disc.
#   · Joins, at vertex V with offsets n_in / n_out (length hw, the segment rects' own corners):
#     collinear continuation -> nothing (the rects meet edge to edge); ROUND -> the disc; BEVEL ->
#     triangle (V, V + s*n_in, V + s*n_out) on the side the offsets diverge; MITER -> that
#     triangle plus (V + s*n_in, tip, V + s*n_out), or the bevel alone past the limit. A full 180°
#     reversal is an infinite miter and a zero-area bevel: a flat, butt-like end.
#   · ⚠ INSIDE A CURVE THE JOIN STYLE DOES NOT APPLY. A vertex the flattener put inside one quad or
#     cubic stands in for a smooth curve, whose stroke is the sweep of a disc: it mitres while the
#     tip stays within the flatten tolerance of that disc — ratio <= 1 + 0.25 px / hw — and takes
#     the round join past it. A MITER join there at the user's limit drew the cusp of
#     "M 10,30 C 50,5 10,5 50,30" (a 150° flattened turn, ratio 3.86 < 4) as a 6 px spike on a 4 px
#     stroke, MEASURED; now it is round. The style applies wherever two VERBS meet, and at a closed
#     subpath's start.
#   · miter_limit is a 16.16 RATIO, SVG `stroke-miterlimit`: miterLength / strokeWidth =
#     1 / sin(theta / 2), theta the INTERIOR angle between the segments; above the limit the join
#     is a bevel, AT the limit it is still a miter. Clamped to [SD_ONE, SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX].
#
# ⚠ THE LIMIT TEST IS INTEGER MATH, WITHOUT OVERFLOW. Each segment direction is rescaled by a power
# of two so its larger component lies in [2^13, 2^14); with P = isqrt(|e1|^2 |e2|^2) < 2^29 and
# D = e1.e2, "ratio > limit" is 2P > L^2 (P + D), tested as L(P + D) < ceil(P * 2^33 / L) — every
# intermediate below 2^62 for any L <= 2^31, at any coordinate magnitude. It is EXACT when both
# rescalings shed only zero bits (pixel and half-pixel geometry) and |e1||e2| is an integer: a
# (25,0) / (7,24) turn is a ratio of exactly 1.25, and the suite asserts both sides of it, as it
# does 377,108 / 377,109 around the irrational sqrt(298)/3. Otherwise the 13 kept bits of each
# direction put the decision within ~0.1 % of the limit at SVG's default 4 (derived, not measured;
# wider toward a reversal).
# ⚠ MAGNITUDES. The offsets rescale each direction first (_sd_seg_normal), so no segment length
# overflows. A miter tip lies at most miter_limit * hw from its vertex, and the walk's
# (bx - ax) * (sy - ay) needs edge extents under ~46,000 px: SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX = 1024.0 keeps
# every tip in range up to hw ~ 45 px, and a join past that ratio turns within 0.11° of a reversal.
# ⭐ ONE CALL IS ONE BATCH (0.7.0). The batch and the run GROW (doubling from the global alloc, like
# the fill's edge list), so however long the path, sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex fills every piece of it
# in one pass. Through 0.6.0 a batch held 8,192 edges (about 800 miter segments) and a longer
# subpath was flushed mid-way — and pieces from different batches meet by MAX, so where a subpath
# longer than a batch CROSSES or returns onto itself that loss was not bounded. MEASURED against the
# 0.6.0 bundle, 60 random self-crossing walks of 700..1,100 segments (64x64, widths 0.3..5.3 px,
# BUTT/SQUARE x MITER/BEVEL) against the same walks as one batch (0.6.0 rebuilt with a 16,384-edge
# cap): 0.6.0 split 20, each more than 3 levels off somewhere, worst 118; 0.7.0 is byte-identical to
# the one batch on all 60, and on 60 more of 2,000..5,000 segments (0.6.0: 60 split, worst 131).
# ⚠ THE FLUSH + RE-EMISSION IS KEPT as the fallback. It runs when SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP is set (a
# memory bound) or when a growth fails for want of memory: the next batch re-emits the stretch before
# the flush (until 2*hw + 3 px of it or 16 segments, whichever comes first), and a closed subpath's
# closing join is followed by the same stretch after its vertex 0, so every seam ALONG the path
# falls where one batch is complete (the suite sweeps rings, wiggles, blobs and gears across lowered
# limits, all within 1 level of one batch) — the MAX loss where a path crosses itself returns with
# it. Out of memory, a stroke degrades to exactly 0.6.0's batches; it drops a piece only if an edge
# arrives at a full array whose growth fails, which the reserve before every unit prevents.
# ⚠ Single-threaded, like the fill: one edge batch and one run for the process.
#
# ⛔⛔ A REFUSED ALLOCATION IS A RETURN CODE, NOT A FAULT AND NOT A SILENT UNDER-DRAW (0.7.2).
# All three public entries — sd_canvas_stroke_path, sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex,
# sd_canvas_stroke_path_dash — return SADISH_ERR_OOM for a refusal at ANY allocation they reach:
# the round stroker's per-piece SdPaths (⚠ and, through 0.8.0, the SdPoints inside them — a piece
# path's points are inline coordinates since 0.9.0, so its three requests are all it makes) and a
# curve's flatten points, all on the consumer's seam; the process-lifetime scratch and its growths (the fill /
# stroke scratch, the batch, the run, the curve flags, the dash piece buffer, the flush's row
# accumulator), all on the global alloc. Closes the STROKE half of
# docs/development/issues/archived/2026-09-15-path-construction-stores-through-a-refused-allocation.md.
# ⭐ MEASURED, the same four calls on a materialised 0.7.1 tree and on this one — 32x32, warm
# scratch, a hook granting K allocations and then refusing (K = 0..4):
#   round stroker, a 2-point line     0.7.1 SIGSEGV rc 139   here SADISH_ERR_OOM, 0 of 27,964 ink
#   round stroker, a closed 2-cubic   0.7.1 SIGSEGV rc 139   here SADISH_ERR_OOM, 0 of 59,303
#   styled BUTT/MITER, that curve     0.7.1 SADISH_OK        here SADISH_ERR_OOM
#   dashed BUTT/MITER, that curve     0.7.1 SADISH_OK        here SADISH_ERR_OOM
# ⛔ AND THE INK IS THE SAME ON BOTH TREES WHEREVER 0.7.1 SURVIVED AT ALL: the styled stroke paints
# 0 / 6,165 / 12,117 / 18,441 / 28,682 of its 59,850 units at K = 0..4 on both, the dashed one
# 0 / 6,166 / 7,978 / 13,453 / 17,329 of 36,422. Only the REPORT changed — a starved stroke draws
# what it always drew, and an unstarved one is byte-identical (agnos refagree, all 200 paths).
# ⚠ WHAT A PARTIAL STROKE LEAVES ON THE CANVAS: whatever it had already drawn. THERE IS NO
# ROLLBACK. The pieces before the refusal are MAX-combined into the coverage exactly as a whole
# stroke's are, so the canvas holds a prefix of the stroke and the code says the picture is wrong;
# a caller that wants all-or-nothing strokes into a scratch canvas, or clears and retries. The
# ROUND stroker stops at the FIRST refusal (each piece is its own allocation + fill pass, and a
# hook that refused one refuses the next); the styled path runs its walk out and reports at the
# end, so its batch is still flushed and its ink is the prefix that fit.
# ⛔ AND A REFUSAL LEAVES NO PROCESS-LIFETIME WRECKAGE. The batch is empty after the flush that
# failed (it is emptied before anything in the flush can fail), its truncation flag is cleared, and
# the run, the curve flags and the fill scratch keep the blocks and capacities they had — so the
# NEXT stroke on a healthy allocator draws exactly what it would have drawn had the refusal never
# happened. programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr asserts that byte for byte at every K of every sweep.
# ⚠ sd_flatten_truncated() is unchanged and still STICKY (it belongs to the last flatten, not to the
# last call — path.cyr says so): a stroke scopes it the way sd_fill_impl does and puts the caller's
# standing verdict back. The RETURN CODE is the per-call witness a stroke did not have before.

# ---- cap / join styles ------------------------------------------
var SD_CAP_BUTT   = 0;
var SD_CAP_ROUND  = 1;
var SD_CAP_SQUARE = 2;

var SD_JOIN_MITER = 0;
var SD_JOIN_ROUND = 1;
var SD_JOIN_BEVEL = 2;

# Upper clamp for the miter limit ratio: 1024.0 in 16.16.
var SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX = 67108864;

# ---- stroking (v0.4.0) -----------------------------------------
# A segment's rectangle: offset both endpoints by `hw` along the segment's
# perpendicular (magnitude via sd_isqrt), fill-union it. Round caps + round
# joins come from a disc at every vertex, so no bevel/miter math is needed.
# Each piece is MAX-unioned so overlaps combine regardless of winding.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM when the piece path or the fill underneath is refused —
# this piece then draws NOTHING (the half-built path is abandoned to an allocator with no free()),
# and every piece already drawn stays on the canvas. ⚠ 3 sd_alloc calls a rect on the consumer's
# seam: the path's record + verbs + points, and NOTHING per corner (0.9.0; 7 through 0.8.0, when
# each corner was a 16 B SdPoint of its own).
#
# ⭐ THE PATH IS OPENED AT ITS EXACT SIZE (0.8.0) — item 2 of
# docs/development/proposals/2026-09-15-path-capacity-for-known-size-paths.md's "Still open": this is
# one of the two in-tree sites that knew its size and bought SD_PATH_CAP = 256 + 256 anyway.
# ⛔ THE COUNTS ARE READ OFF THE FIVE CALLS BELOW, NOT ESTIMATED: moveto + 3 linetos + close = 5
# verbs; moveto and each lineto carry one point and close carries none = 4 points. Both land under
# SD_PATH_CAP_MIN = 8, so each array is opened at 8 and neither can grow here — the path is built
# with 3 VERB SLOTS AND 4 POINT SLOTS SPARE.
# ⚠ THAT SLACK IS REAL, SO ADDING ONE CALL BELOW DOES NOT BUY A DOUBLING — MEASURED, at these same
# arguments: one extra lineto is the SAME 240 B in the SAME 3 sd_allocs (6 verbs, 5 points, both
# capacities still 8), three extra likewise (8 verbs, 7 points, the verb array exactly full), and
# neither array has moved. The verb array first doubles at the NINTH verb — moveto + 7 linetos +
# close — which is 368 B in 4 with vcap 8 -> 16; the point array at the ninth point, one lineto
# later. ⚠ Through 0.8.0 each added lineto also bought a 16 B SdPoint, so those rows read 256 B in
# 8 and 288 B in 10; inline (x, y) makes an added point free until an array actually overflows.
# ⇒ What FAILS when the five calls below become six is group N's COUNT checks in
# programs/path_cap_test.cyr (5 verbs, 4 points), NOT its capacity checks: those do not move until
# the fourth extra lineto. Group N8 pins the whole boundary, so these numbers stay honest.
# ⭐ MEASURED on the seam, per call: 4,208 B (0.7.2) -> 240 B (0.8.0) -> 240 B in THREE allocations
# (0.9.0) — the same bytes, re-spent: 48 + 64 of verbs + 128 of INLINE points, where 0.8.0 paid
# 48 + 64 + 64 plus four 16 B SdPoints in seven calls. The closed 8x8 rect stroke 34,432 ->
# 3,232 -> 2,720 B, and 104 -> 24 sd_alloc calls (programs/path_cap_test.cyr group N).
fn sd_stroke_seg(cv, ax, ay, bx, by, hw): i64 {
    var dx = bx - ax;
    var dy = by - ay;
    var len2 = dx * dx + dy * dy;
    if (len2 == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }     # zero-length -> the vertex disc covers it
    var l = sd_isqrt(len2);          # length in 16.16
    if (l == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ox = ((0 - dy) * hw) / l;    # perpendicular offset, magnitude hw
    var oy = (dx * hw) / l;
    var rp = sd_path_new_cap(5, 4);        # 5 verbs, 4 points — the five calls below, counted
    if (rp == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_moveto(rp, ax + ox, ay + oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, bx + ox, by + oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, bx - ox, by - oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_lineto(rp, ax - ox, ay - oy) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_close(rp) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return sd_canvas_fill_union(cv, rp, SD_FILL_NONZERO);
}

# A radius-hw disc (16-gon) at (cx, cy) — round cap / round join filler.
# SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM on a refusal (nothing of this disc drawn), as sd_stroke_seg.
# ⚠ 3 sd_alloc calls a disc — the path's three, and nothing per vertex (0.9.0; 19 through 0.8.0,
# when each of the sixteen vertices was an SdPoint on the seam).
#
# ⭐ EXACT CAPACITY, LIKE sd_stroke_seg (0.8.0) — the second of the proposal's two in-tree sites.
# ⛔ THE 17 AND THE 16 ARE THE `while (k < 16)` BELOW, COUNTED: 16 iterations emit one moveto and 15
# linetos, then sd_path_close adds the 17th verb and no point — so 17 verbs and 16 points, and the
# two arrays are EXACTLY full when the disc is built. ⚠ That is the tightest fit in the tree, and a
# capacity one slot short of it pays a doubling and a memcpy: MEASURED, this shape opened at (16, 16)
# is 688 B in 4 sd_alloc calls against 440 B in 3 — still well under the 4,400 B the 256-slot
# default cost, and still 248 B and one copy more than deriving the count costs. ⚠ Raise the 16 (and
# the `411775 / 16` step with it) without moving these arguments and BOTH arrays double.
# programs/path_cap_test.cyr group N reads the capacities AND the counts back off the record, so a
# changed 16 fails a check instead of quietly buying a doubling.
# ⭐ MEASURED on the seam, per call: 4,400 B (0.7.2) -> 568 B (0.8.0) -> 440 B in THREE allocations
# (0.9.0): 48 + 136 of verbs + 256 of INLINE points, where 0.8.0 paid 48 + 136 + 128 plus sixteen
# 16 B SdPoints in nineteen calls.
fn sd_stroke_disc(cv, cx, cy, hw): i64 {
    var dp = sd_path_new_cap(17, 16);      # 16 vertices + the close = 17 verbs, 16 points
    if (dp == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    var step = 411775 / 16;          # 2*pi / 16 in 16.16
    var k = 0;
    var vrc = SADISH_OK;
    while (k < 16) {
        if (vrc == SADISH_OK) {
            var ang = k * step;
            var px = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, sd_cos(ang));
            var py = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, sd_sin(ang));
            if (k == 0) { vrc = sd_path_moveto(dp, px, py); } else { vrc = sd_path_lineto(dp, px, py); }
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (vrc != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (sd_path_close(dp) != SADISH_OK) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return sd_canvas_fill_union(cv, dp, SD_FILL_NONZERO);
}

# Stroke one flattened subpath run: a rect per segment + a disc per vertex.
# ⚠ `run` HOLDS COORDINATES, 16 B A POINT — x at +0, y at +8 (0.9.0; an array of 8 B SdPoint
# POINTERS through 0.8.0). The run is filled from a path's inline points and from a flatten's
# output, and copying the two numbers out of a flatten's SdPoint is what keeps a straight verb's
# anchor from needing a record of its own. See _sd_stroke_run in src/raster.cyr.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or the first SADISH_ERR_OOM a piece reported — and STOPS there: the remaining
# pieces are not attempted, because each is another allocation on the hook that has just refused
# one. What was drawn stays drawn (the header's no-rollback rule).
fn sd_stroke_run(cv, run, n, closed, hw): i64 {
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n - 1) {
        var src = sd_stroke_seg(cv, load64(run + i * 16), load64(run + i * 16 + 8),
          load64(run + (i + 1) * 16), load64(run + (i + 1) * 16 + 8), hw);
        if (src != SADISH_OK) { return src; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (n > 1) {
            var crc = sd_stroke_seg(cv, load64(run + (n - 1) * 16), load64(run + (n - 1) * 16 + 8),
              load64(run + 0), load64(run + 8), hw);
            if (crc != SADISH_OK) { return crc; }
        }
    }
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var drc = sd_stroke_disc(cv, load64(run + k * 16), load64(run + k * 16 + 8), hw);
        if (drc != SADISH_OK) { return drc; }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# The round stroker's verb walk — sd_canvas_stroke_path's body, split out (0.7.2) so the entry can
# put the caller's standing flatten verdict back on the way out of an early return. Returns
# SADISH_OK, or the first SADISH_ERR_OOM a piece reported, which STOPS the walk (sd_stroke_run).
fn _sd_round_walk(cv, path, hw): i64 {
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    var tol = SD_ONE >> 2;
    var run_n = 0;
    var fbuf = _sd_stroke_fbuf;
    var fctx = _sd_stroke_fctx;

    # ⛔ COORDINATES AND A `has_cur` FLAG (0.9.0) — a path's points are inline now, so the current
    # point is two numbers and 0 is the ORIGIN rather than "no moveto yet". Same flag, same reason,
    # as src/raster.cyr's fill walk and src/path.cyr's flatten walk.
    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var closed = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            if (run_n > 0) {
                var mrc = sd_stroke_run(cv, _sd_stroke_run, run_n, closed, hw);
                if (mrc != SADISH_OK) { return mrc; }
            }
            run_n = 0;
            closed = 0;
            curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
            cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
            has_cur = 1;
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16, curx);
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8, cury);
            run_n = run_n + 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a drawing verb before any moveto is skipped (raster.cyr)
                curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
                cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
                run_n = _sd_run_push(run_n, curx, cury, 0);
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                var qex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1);
                var qey = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1);
                _sd_flatten_quad_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), qex, qey, 0, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 0);
                curx = qex;
                cury = qey;
            }
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a curve before any moveto is skipped (see raster.cyr)
                var kex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 2);
                var key = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 2);
                _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
                  sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), kex, key, 0, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
                run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 0);
                curx = kex;
                cury = key;
            }
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CLOSE) {
            closed = 1;
        }
        vi = vi + 1;
    }
    if (run_n > 0) { return sd_stroke_run(cv, _sd_stroke_run, run_n, closed, hw); }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_stroke_path — stroke `path` with the given `width` (16.16) onto
# `cv`'s coverage, with round caps + round joins. Flattens curves, then per
# subpath draws a rectangle per segment and a disc per vertex, MAX-combined
# into the coverage buffer.
#
# Returns SADISH_OK; SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for cv == 0; SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a 0 or verb-less
# path; SADISH_ERR_OOM when any allocation the stroke reaches is refused — the piece paths and
# their points on the seam, a curve's flatten mid-points, or the process-lifetime scratch. A
# width <= 0 draws nothing and is SADISH_OK. ⚠ An OOM leaves the pieces already drawn ON the
# canvas: see the no-rollback block in this file's header.
#
# Styled caps/joins are sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex below; this entry stays the
# 0.4.0 piece-per-pass stroker, byte-identical.
# TODO(v0.5): batch the round pieces — this still allocs a PATH (on the seam)
# + runs a fill pass per segment/vertex (correctness-first); the fixed scratch
# underneath is process-lifetime (0.5.5).
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    if (n_verbs == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var hw = sd_asr(width, 1);
    if (hw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }

    # The stroker's OWN scratch set — the run stays live across the fill_union passes below, which
    # use the fill set (see the scratch block in raster.cyr). ⚠ The run GROWS (0.7.0), so it is
    # read as _sd_stroke_run at each use, never cached across a push.
    _sd_scratch_init();
    # ⛔ THE GUARD IS ON THE BLOCK THE WALK STORES THROUGH, not on _sd_scratch_init's status —
    # sd_fill_impl's reasoning (see it), applied to the stroker's own run: the publish is
    # all-or-nothing, so a 0 here covers both a refused set and anything that ever published a 0
    # behind a status of 1. Through 0.7.1 the status was discarded and this 0 was stored through.
    if (_sd_stroke_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_run_trunc = 0;
    # The flatten's truncation verdict, SCOPED TO THIS CALL exactly as sd_fill_impl scopes it: from
    # here a 1 means THIS stroke lost mid-points. The caller's standing verdict is put back below,
    # so sd_flatten_truncated() answers what it always answered (path.cyr: sticky until the next
    # sd_path_flatten) and the return code is what answers for this call.
    var flat_was = _sd_flat_trunc;
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;

    var rc = _sd_round_walk(cv, path, hw);

    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) {
        if (rc == SADISH_OK) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    }
    # ⛔ AND THE CALLER'S STANDING VERDICT GOES BACK — the line _sd_round_walk was split out FOR.
    # Without it a stroke would CLEAR a verdict its caller was holding from an earlier
    # sd_path_flatten, which path.cyr documents as sticky until the next flatten. MEASURED as a
    # mutation (this line deleted): a starved sd_path_flatten leaves sd_flatten_truncated() at 1, a
    # healthy sd_canvas_stroke_path then reads it back 0. Gated by stroke_oom_test checks 115-117,
    # the round twin of the _ex pair at 112-114.
    if (flat_was != 0) { _sd_flat_trunc = 1; }
    # A run point the growth could not take is ink the caller asked for and did not get, exactly as
    # a lost mid-point is (_sd_run_grow / _sd_inner_grow set it; raster.cyr's _sd_grow_limit and a
    # real out-of-memory are the two ways to reach it).
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) {
        if (rc == SADISH_OK) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    }
    return rc;
}

# ---- styled stroking: the edge batch -----------------------------
# Edges one batch may hold before it is flushed. ⭐ 0 — THE DEFAULT (0.7.0) — IS NO LIMIT: the batch
# grows, so one sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex call is ONE fill however long its path. A positive value
# forces a flush past that many edges (the flush + re-emission below, which is otherwise the
# out-of-memory fallback) and so bounds the batch's memory: it is held to
# [SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN, SD_FLATTEN_CAP] when the FIRST styled stroke sizes the array
# (_sd_sb_cap_for), so a consumer that wants a small batch pays for a small one — MEASURED, a first
# styled stroke at 900: 37,248 B against the default's 270,592 B. Through 0.6.0 this was 8,192 and a
# value raised AFTER the first stroke was held to the allocation; now the array grows to meet it.
# Setting 8192 reproduces 0.6.0's batches exactly.
# ⚠ It bounds the batch, not every array a stroke touches: the run and the curve flags are sized by
# the PATH (raster.cyr's scratch), and the crossings the flush borrows follow the batch.
var SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 0;
# Room reserved before one unit. A unit is at most 39 edges: a join (a 16-gon disc, or 3 bevel + 16
# for a curve vertex past its tolerance), the rect (4), and an end cap or the closing join (16).
var SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES = 40;
# The smallest limit that holds what may follow a flush without another: 16 re-emitted units back
# (<= 23 edges each — never a last unit), the unit, and a closed subpath's 16 forward: 775 edges.
# ⚠ Since 0.7.0 a lower limit costs flushes, not ink: an edge arriving past the limit grows the
# array rather than being dropped (_sd_sb_edge), so a starved re-emission is emitted in full and
# only the NEXT unit flushes. MEASURED: a 2,000-sample Lissajous at a limit of 10 strokes the same
# bytes as at 800 (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr) — at about 17x the fills.
var SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN = 800;

# ⛔ Process-lifetime, from the GLOBAL alloc, allocated by the first styled stroke — not by
# _sd_scratch_init, so a process that never strokes with styles never pays for it. Same reason as
# raster.cyr's scratch: a consumer's hook is an arena that gets reset. MEASURED one-time cost
# 270,592 B (8,192 * 32 edges + 256 trig + 8,192 curve flags) at the default SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP,
# and only the edge part of it under a lower one (MEASURED at 900: 37,248 B); after it a
# straight-line styled stroke costs the heap 0 B, and curves only their flatten mid-points. The
# flush's crossings use the fill's _sd_fill_cross: a per-fill working array, not held across a call,
# which the flush IS — so it is borrowed, not duplicated.
# ⭐ The edges and the curve flags GROW by doubling (0.7.0), from the global alloc, like the fill's
# list: MEASURED 524,288 B for the first batch past 8,192 edges, plus 262,144 B of crossings unless a
# fill already grew them (every growth keeps _sd_fill_cross_cap >= _sd_sb_cap); a repeat 0 B.
# ⚠ AND IT IS MONOTONE, AND NEVER RETURNED (lib/alloc.cyr has no free): one batch per call means the
# batch is sized by the LONGEST path the process ever strokes, and every abandoned doubling stays
# mapped. MEASURED, random walks of 100..25,600 segments at width 3 (BUTT/MITER, a styled stroke
# makes 7-20 edges a segment): the batch settles at 262,144 edges and the process has asked the
# global heap for 25,682,744 B in total, once — against 1,303,352 B over the same sweep with
# SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 8192 (of which 835,584 B is the run and its parallel flags, which 0.6.0
# truncated instead). ⇒ The knob is the ceiling for a consumer that needs one; it bounds the first
# allocation too.
# ⚠ THE RUN'S SHARE WAS 442,368 B THROUGH 0.8.0 and is RE-MEASURED here for 0.9.0's 16 B inline
# point: it is the doubling LADDER the longest walk forces (8,192 -> 16,384 -> 32,768 slots at
# 16 B, plus the flags' 16,384 + 32,768 B), not the walk, so it reproduces from any generator that
# reaches 25,601 points — verified against 0.8.0's own src, which returns the 442,368 above to the
# byte. The initial 8,192-slot block is NOT in that figure; it is in _sd_scratch_init's one-time
# cost (131,072 B, 65,536 through 0.8.0). ⭐ The sweep's TOTAL barely moved even so: the same walks'
# PATHS cost 408,800 B less on the seam (51,100 points x the 8 B pointer that is gone), so the run's
# +393,216 B of global heap is very nearly paid for by the caller's own arena — MEASURED +49,952 B
# net across the nine walks.
var _sd_sb_edges = 0;   # _sd_sb_cap * 32 — (ax, ay, bx, by) per edge, a -> b in winding order
var _sd_sb_cap = 0;     # edges the array holds
var _sd_sb_n = 0;       # edges in the batch
var _sd_sb_trig = 0;    # 16 (cos, sin) pairs — the 16-gon disc, sd_stroke_disc's angles
# _sd_sb_inner is never read at run[0]: a subpath's first point takes a cap, or the closing join,
# which is passed 0.
var _sd_sb_inner = 0;   # _sd_sb_inner_cap bytes, parallel to the run: 1 = inside a curve's flattening
var _sd_sb_inner_cap = 0;
var _sd_sb_trunc = 0;   # 1 if the batch dropped an edge (a growth failed); cleared by the flush
var _sd_run_trunc = 0;  # 1 if THIS stroke call dropped a run point (a growth failed)
# ⛔ 1 if something INSIDE the styled machinery could not get memory during this call (0.7.2): the
# flush's row accumulator or the area engine's, a batch that dropped an edge, a dash piece the
# buffer could not take. It is what carries a refusal from a place that cannot return one — a
# mid-walk _sd_sb_flush, a _sd_dash_put four frames down — out to the public entry point.
# ⚠ Cleared at the top of _sd_styled_walk, which is the ONLY reader, and the only path on which any
# of those sites runs. The round stroker reports through return codes instead and never sets it.
var _sd_stroke_oom = 0;

# The edge array's INITIAL capacity, from SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP: 0 (no limit) takes the full
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP, a positive limit is held to [SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN, SD_FLATTEN_CAP]. ⛔ Never more
# than SD_FLATTEN_CAP, which is what _sd_scratch_init sized the borrowed crossings to — n edges make
# at most n crossings, so the crossings >= batch invariant holds from the first stroke. A limit
# raised later is met by growing (_sd_sb_room), which reserves the crossings first.
fn _sd_sb_cap_for(): i64 {
    var cap = SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP;
    if (cap <= 0) { return SD_FLATTEN_CAP; }
    if (cap < SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN) { cap = SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN; }
    if (cap > SD_FLATTEN_CAP) { cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP; }
    return cap;
}

# Allocate the batch, the curve flags and the trig table once (idempotent). Returns 1 when they are
# ready, 0 when a block was refused.
# ⛔ ALL OR NOTHING, AND `_sd_sb_edges != 0` IS THE FLAG — _sd_scratch_init's rule, for the same
# reason (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 the three blocks went straight into the globals unchecked: a refused
# trig table faulted in the table write two lines below it, and a refused `inner` or `edges` was
# published WITH ITS CAPACITY, so the first curve flag or the first edge of that very stroke was
# written through address 0. The blocks now land in locals and are published together, so a partial
# set is abandoned (no free()) and the next call retries from scratch.
# ⚠ These come from the GLOBAL `alloc`, never sd_alloc (raster.cyr's scratch rule), so no consumer
# hook can refuse them: the only honest way to drive this path is the CAPACITY, which sizes two of
# the three — programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr raises SD_FLATTEN_CAP past ALLOC_MAX / 8, the device
# oom_test group J uses on the fill scratch.
# ⛔ AND THESE THREE 0-TESTS ARE GATES, NOT A CONTRACT — `_sd_sb_edges` IS NOT ALWAYS THE LARGEST
# BLOCK OF THE SET, unlike _sd_scratch_init's eight (where every capacity-sized block is CAP*8 or
# more, so a size-based refusal takes them together). Here `inner` is SD_FLATTEN_CAP BYTES while
# `edges` is _sd_sb_cap_for() * 32, and _sd_sb_cap_for reads SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP — the PUBLIC knob
# declared above. They move together only while that knob is 0 (then cap == SD_FLATTEN_CAP and
# edges is 32x the larger); set it, and edges shrinks to it while inner does not. So whenever
# SD_FLATTEN_CAP > 32 * cap, `inner` is the LARGEST block and lib/alloc.cyr refuses IT while
# granting `edges` — the one arrangement a partial publish cannot survive, because `_sd_sb_edges`
# is also the idempotence flag.
# ⛔ MEASURED, SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP = 1000 with SD_FLATTEN_CAP = 3,000,000,000: this function
# reverted to 0.7.1's unchecked stores publishes edges = <32,000 B block>, inner = 0,
# inner_cap = 3,000,000,000 and returns 1; the caller's `_sd_sb_edges == 0` guard passes, and the
# next _sd_run_push writes its curve flag through address 0 — SIGSEGV, rc 139. And because
# `_sd_sb_edges != 0` keeps this function from ever running again, so does EVERY styled stroke for
# the rest of the process: the 0.7.0 poisoned-scratch shape, exactly.
# ⇒ programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr group G4 drives those two values and gates all five publishes.
# ⚠ Those two values are how a SUITE reaches it; a real machine reaches it without any knob at all,
# because lib/alloc.cyr also refuses when the heap cannot be extended, and then any one of the three
# can be the one that fails. The size argument is what makes the path testable, not what makes the
# tests necessary.
fn _sd_sb_init(): i64 {
    if (_sd_sb_edges != 0) { return 1; }
    var trig = alloc(16 * 16);
    var inner = alloc(SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
    var cap = _sd_sb_cap_for();
    var edges = alloc(cap * 32);
    var ok = 1;
    if (trig == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (inner == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (edges == 0) { ok = 0; }
    if (ok == 0) { return 0; }
    var step = 411775 / 16;          # 2*pi / 16 in 16.16, as sd_stroke_disc
    var k = 0;
    while (k < 16) {
        store64(trig + k * 16, sd_cos(k * step));
        store64(trig + k * 16 + 8, sd_sin(k * step));
        k = k + 1;
    }
    _sd_sb_trig = trig;
    _sd_sb_inner = inner;
    _sd_sb_inner_cap = SD_FLATTEN_CAP;
    _sd_sb_cap = cap;
    _sd_sb_edges = edges;
    return 1;
}

# Grow the batch to hold at least `need` edges: crossings first, then the edges, doubling, the live
# ones copied and the old block abandoned. Returns 1, or 0 if an allocation failed (nothing lost).
fn _sd_sb_grow(need): i64 {
    var nc = _sd_sb_cap;
    while (nc < need) { nc = nc * 2; }
    if (_sd_fill_cross_for(nc) == 0) { return 0; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 32);
    if (blk == 0) { return 0; }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_sb_edges, _sd_sb_n * 32);
    _sd_sb_edges = blk;
    _sd_sb_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# 1 if `k` more edges may go into the batch: within SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP (when set) and within the
# array, grown if need be. 0 tells the caller to flush first — a limit reached, or a growth that
# failed, which is how a stroke degrades out of memory: to 0.6.0's batches, not to lost pieces.
fn _sd_sb_room(k): i64 {
    var need = _sd_sb_n + k;
    var lim = SD_STROKE_BATCH_CAP;
    if (lim > 0) {
        if (lim < SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN) { lim = SD_STROKE_BATCH_MIN; }
        if (need > lim) { return 0; }
    }
    if (need > _sd_sb_cap) { return _sd_sb_grow(need); }
    return 1;
}

# Make the stroker's run hold `n` points — and, when `flags` != 0, the curve flags too — growing
# either by doubling from the global alloc (live entries copied). Returns 1, or 0 when a growth
# failed: the caller drops the point, as 0.6.0 did at 8,192, and _sd_run_trunc records it; this call
# then stops trying to grow, so a subpath keeps a prefix of its points.
fn _sd_run_fits(n, flags): i64 {
    if (n > _sd_stroke_run_cap) {
        if (_sd_run_grow(n) == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    if (flags != 0) {
        if (n > _sd_sb_inner_cap) {
            if (_sd_inner_grow(n) == 0) { return 0; }
        }
    }
    return 1;
}

# ⚠ 16 B A SLOT (0.9.0; 8 B a pointer through 0.8.0), so a growth of the same capacity asks for
# twice the bytes — and the COPY is still the whole old CAPACITY, as _sd_inner_grow's header
# explains for the flags.
fn _sd_run_grow(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = _sd_stroke_run_cap;
    while (nc < n) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc * 16);
    if (blk == 0) {
        _sd_run_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_stroke_run_cap * 16);
    _sd_stroke_run = blk;
    _sd_stroke_run_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# ⚠ THE COPY IS THE WHOLE OLD CAPACITY, not the run's live count, and deliberately: _sd_run_append
# grows the flags at the point it needs slot n, and a curve's flags are written AFTERWARDS by
# _sd_sb_mark_curve — so the byte at the old capacity's last index is, mid-curve, about to be
# rewritten and, on a LINETO, already 0. A short copy is therefore invisible today only because
# lib/alloc.cyr hands out freshly-mapped (zero) pages and never recycles memory; under an allocator
# that reused a block it would be a wrong-join bug. Copying the capacity costs one pass over at most
# the flags' own size and needs neither of those arguments.
# (programs/grow_edges_test.cyr writes a 1 into the last slot before a growth and reads it back.)
fn _sd_inner_grow(n): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
    var nc = _sd_sb_inner_cap;
    while (nc < n) { nc = nc * 2; }
    var blk = _sd_grow_alloc(nc);
    if (blk == 0) {
        _sd_run_trunc = 1;
        return 0;
    }
    memcpy(blk, _sd_sb_inner, _sd_sb_inner_cap);
    _sd_sb_inner = blk;
    _sd_sb_inner_cap = nc;
    return 1;
}

# Append a flatten ctx's points to the stroker's run (_sd_stroke_run), growing it — and, when
# `flags` != 0, the styled path's parallel curve flags — as needed; a point that still does not fit
# is dropped (see _sd_run_fits). Returns the new count. (0.7.0: was sd_stroke_append(run, run_n,
# fctx), bounded by SD_FLATTEN_CAP; the run pointer is no longer a parameter because growth
# replaces the block.)
# ⚠ THE CTX'S POINTS ARE STILL SdPoints AND THE RUN IS NOT (0.9.0): each emitted point's two
# coordinates are COPIED into the run's 16 B slot, so nothing in the run points at anything. That is
# what lets a flatten's per-curve buffer be rewound under a run that is still being stroked.
fn _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, flags): i64 {
    var pts = load64(fctx + 0);
    var n = load64(fctx + 8);
    var i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        if (_sd_run_fits(run_n + 1, flags) != 0) {
            var p = load64(pts + i * 8);
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16, sd_point_x(p));
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8, sd_point_y(p));
            run_n = run_n + 1;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return run_n;
}

# Append the point (x, y) to the run (a LINETO); with `flags` != 0 its curve flag is cleared too.
fn _sd_run_push(run_n, x, y, flags): i64 {
    if (_sd_run_fits(run_n + 1, flags) == 0) { return run_n; }
    store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16, x);
    store64(_sd_stroke_run + run_n * 16 + 8, y);
    if (flags != 0) { store8(_sd_sb_inner + run_n, 0); }
    return run_n + 1;
}

# Append the edge a -> b. Horizontal edges cross no sub-scanline and are dropped. The unit reserve
# (_sd_sb_room) has already made room; if an edge still arrives at a full array it grows here, and
# only if THAT fails is the edge dropped — and _sd_sb_trunc set, so the flush does not hand an
# incomplete list to the area engine. Never a write past the array.
fn _sd_sb_edge(ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    if (ay == by) { return 0; }
    if (_sd_sb_n >= _sd_sb_cap) {
        if (_sd_sb_trunc != 0) { return 0; }
        if (_sd_sb_grow(_sd_sb_n + 1) == 0) {
            _sd_sb_trunc = 1;
            return 0;
        }
    }
    var e = _sd_sb_edges + _sd_sb_n * 32;
    store64(e + 0, ax);
    store64(e + 8, ay);
    store64(e + 16, bx);
    store64(e + 24, by);
    _sd_sb_n = _sd_sb_n + 1;
    return 0;
}

# Fill the batch into `cv` — MAX-combined, through the clip — and empty it. The default fill's
# sampling (SD_FILL_SS sub-scanlines, analytic x, sd_fill_impl's crossing formula and sort), but
# the nonzero walk opens a span where the winding leaves 0 and closes it where it returns, so
# overlapping pieces are one span and one floor. ⚠ And where the winding returns to 0 and the next
# crossing reopens within 1 LSB, the span carries on: two pieces that only TOUCH (a join
# triangle's side lies along the rect's end edge; consecutive rects share one) compute that shared
# line's crossing from different endpoints, each truncation under 1 LSB off the true x — so they
# differ by at most 1, and a split there would floor the pixel to 254 all over again (MEASURED,
# a 40-gon ring at width 5 under MITER: 53 pixels at 254 without the bridge; with it 2, both on the
# band's rim, where the round stroker reads 254 too).
# Bridging a real 1-LSB gap adds 1/4 of a raw unit of coverage. Rows outside the batch's y range
# are skipped: they would MAX a 0 into the coverage.
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM when the row accumulator (or the area engine's) is refused —
# and sets _sd_stroke_oom either way, because the flushes _sd_sb_subpath makes mid-walk have no
# caller that can return anything (0.7.2).
# ⛔ THE COUNT IS TAKEN AND THE BATCH EMPTIED (`_sd_sb_n = 0`, two lines into the body) BEFORE
# ANYTHING HERE CAN FAIL, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING — it is what makes a refused flush cost the
# caller a picture and not the process. An accumulator refused with the batch still full would leave
# the failed stroke's pieces sitting in it, and the NEXT styled stroke on a healthy allocator would
# fill them together with its own: MEASURED as a mutation (the accrow guard below putting the count
# back), a refused dashed stroke of the closed curve leaves 270 edges in the batch, and the next
# healthy stroke — of the straight line, a different shape — comes back with 261 of its 1,024 pixels
# carrying the curve's ink. This function must therefore keep every early return AFTER that line.
# programs/stroke_oom_test.cyr checks 43-45 gate it.
fn _sd_sb_flush(cv): i64 {
    var n = _sd_sb_n;
    var trunc = _sd_sb_trunc;
    _sd_sb_trunc = 0;
    # An edge the batch could not take is ink the caller asked for and did not get: report it here,
    # where the batch that lost it is consumed. _sd_sb_edge sets the flag only after a growth failed.
    if (trunc != 0) { _sd_stroke_oom = 1; }
    if (n == 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    _sd_sb_n = 0;
    var edges = _sd_sb_edges;
    var cross = _sd_fill_cross;         # the CURRENT block: _sd_sb_grow keeps its capacity >= n
    var w = sd_canvas_width(cv);
    var h = sd_canvas_height(cv);
    var cov = sd_canvas_coverage(cv);
    var stride = sd_canvas_stride(cv);
    # ⛔ SD_AA_AREA canvas: the exact engine, nonzero, MAX-combined through the clip. Every piece is
    # a closed polygon, so the list is whole unless _sd_sb_edge dropped an edge — which since 0.7.0
    # it records (_sd_sb_trunc), and only after a growth failed. A truncated batch takes the walk
    # below (a dropped edge would streak the area engine to the right edge). Through 0.6.0 a batch
    # AT its limit could not be told from a truncated one and always took the walk.
    if (sd_canvas_aa(cv) == SD_AA_AREA) {
        if (trunc == 0) {
            var mask = 0;
            var clipn = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
            if (clipn != 0) { mask = load64(clipn + 0); }
            # ⚠ SADISH_ERR_OOM out of here is the area engine's own (w + 1) * 8 accumulator being
            # refused (src/coverage.cyr); it writes no coverage when that happens.
            var arc = _sd_area_fill(edges, n, w, h, cov, stride, mask, 1, 1);
            if (arc != SADISH_OK) { _sd_stroke_oom = 1; }
            return arc;
        }
    }
    var ymin = load64(edges + 8);
    var ymax = ymin;
    var ei = 0;
    while (ei < n) {
        var ey0 = load64(edges + ei * 32 + 8);
        var ey1 = load64(edges + ei * 32 + 24);
        if (ey0 < ymin) { ymin = ey0; }
        if (ey1 < ymin) { ymin = ey1; }
        if (ey0 > ymax) { ymax = ey0; }
        if (ey1 > ymax) { ymax = ey1; }
        ei = ei + 1;
    }
    var py = sd_asr(ymin, SD_SHIFT);
    if (py < 0) { py = 0; }
    var py_end = sd_asr(ymax, SD_SHIFT) + 1;
    if (py_end > h) { py_end = h; }
    var ssy = SD_FILL_SS;
    var weight = SD_ONE / ssy;
    var accrow = _sd_fill_accrow_for(w);
    # ⛔ A REFUSED ACCUMULATOR IS REPORTED, NOT STORED THROUGH (0.7.2). Through 0.7.1 this 0 went
    # straight into the row loop below and killed the process — raster.cyr's _sd_fill_accrow_for
    # named this caller as the one that did not test it. The block comes from the GLOBAL alloc and
    # is only ever refused for a canvas wider than the allocator can serve a w*8 row for (MEASURED,
    # a hand-built 536,870,912 px header: w*8 = 4 GiB, over lib/alloc.cyr's ALLOC_MAX).
    # ⛔ THE BATCH IS ALREADY EMPTY HERE AND THIS RETURN MUST NOT PUT IT BACK — the header block on
    # this function says why (the next stroke would paint this one's pieces). The lost pieces are
    # the under-draw the code tells the caller about, and no coverage byte has been touched.
    if (accrow == 0) {
        _sd_stroke_oom = 1;
        return SADISH_ERR_OOM;
    }
    while (py < py_end) {
        var zi = 0;
        while (zi < w) {
            store64(accrow + zi * 8, 0);
            zi = zi + 1;
        }
        var s = 0;
        while (s < ssy) {
            var sy = (py << SD_SHIFT) + (s * SD_ONE + (SD_ONE >> 1)) / ssy;
            var nc = 0;
            ei = 0;
            while (ei < n) {
                var e = edges + ei * 32;
                var ay = load64(e + 8);
                var by = load64(e + 24);
                var ylo = ay;
                var yhi = by;
                var dir = 1;
                if (ay > by) {
                    ylo = by;
                    yhi = ay;
                    dir = 0 - 1;
                }
                if (sy >= ylo) {
                    if (sy < yhi) {
                        var ax = load64(e + 0);
                        var bx = load64(e + 16);
                        store64(cross + nc * 16, ax + ((bx - ax) * (sy - ay)) / (by - ay));
                        store64(cross + nc * 16 + 8, dir);
                        nc = nc + 1;
                    }
                }
                ei = ei + 1;
            }
            var a = 1;
            while (a < nc) {
                var kx = load64(cross + a * 16);
                var kd = load64(cross + a * 16 + 8);
                var b = a - 1;
                var stop = 0;
                while (stop == 0) {
                    if (b < 0) {
                        stop = 1;
                    } else {
                        if (load64(cross + b * 16) > kx) {
                            store64(cross + (b + 1) * 16, load64(cross + b * 16));
                            store64(cross + (b + 1) * 16 + 8, load64(cross + b * 16 + 8));
                            b = b - 1;
                        } else {
                            stop = 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
                store64(cross + (b + 1) * 16, kx);
                store64(cross + (b + 1) * 16 + 8, kd);
                a = a + 1;
            }
            var wind = 0;
            var open = 0;
            var xs = 0;
            var ci = 0;
            while (ci < nc) {
                var cx = load64(cross + ci * 16);
                if (open == 0) {
                    xs = cx;
                    open = 1;
                }
                wind = wind + load64(cross + ci * 16 + 8);
                if (wind == 0) {
                    var bridge = 0;
                    if (ci + 1 < nc) {
                        if (load64(cross + (ci + 1) * 16) - cx <= 1) { bridge = 1; }
                    }
                    if (bridge == 0) {
                        sd_span_add(accrow, w, xs, cx, weight);
                        open = 0;
                    }
                }
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
            s = s + 1;
        }
        var px = 0;
        while (px < w) {
            var c = (load64(accrow + px * 8) * 255) >> SD_SHIFT;
            if (c > 255) { c = 255; }
            var clipnode = load64(cv + SD_CANVAS_CLIP_OFFSET);
            if (clipnode != 0) {
                # ⛔ PACKED `py * w + px` — the clip mask is a w*h block, not a window into a wider
                # buffer, so the canvas stride is not its pitch (raster.cyr's SdCanvas layout note).
                c = (c * load8(load64(clipnode + 0) + py * w + px)) / 255;
            }
            var oldc = load8(cov + py * stride + px);   # coverage: the STRIDE, not w
            if (c < oldc) { c = oldc; }
            store8(cov + py * stride + px, c);
            px = px + 1;
        }
        py = py + 1;
    }
    return SADISH_OK;
}

# The rect A+o, B+o, B-o, A-o — sd_stroke_seg's corners. With o = perp(B - A) * hw / |B - A| its
# shoelace sum is negative for every direction: the winding every batch piece is given.
fn _sd_sb_rect(ax, ay, bx, by, ox, oy): i64 {
    if (ox == 0) {
        if (oy == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    _sd_sb_edge(ax + ox, ay + oy, bx + ox, by + oy);
    _sd_sb_edge(bx + ox, by + oy, bx - ox, by - oy);
    _sd_sb_edge(bx - ox, by - oy, ax - ox, ay - oy);
    return _sd_sb_edge(ax - ox, ay - oy, ax + ox, ay + oy);
}

# max(|a|, |b|).
fn _sd_max_abs(a, b): i64 {
    var m = sd_abs(a);
    if (sd_abs(b) > m) { m = sd_abs(b); }
    return m;
}

# Sign (-1, 0, 1) of cross((ax, ay), (bx, by)). The four components are halved together until
# they are below 2^30, so the products cannot overflow at any magnitude.
fn _sd_cross_sign(ax, ay, bx, by): i64 {
    var a = ax;
    var b = ay;
    var c = bx;
    var d = by;
    while (_sd_max_abs(_sd_max_abs(a, b), _sd_max_abs(c, d)) >= 1073741824) {
        a = sd_asr(a, 1);
        b = sd_asr(b, 1);
        c = sd_asr(c, 1);
        d = sd_asr(d, 1);
    }
    var cr = a * d - b * c;
    if (cr < 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (cr > 0) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# A triangle, wound like the rect (negative shoelace) whatever order its corners arrive in;
# zero area emits nothing.
fn _sd_sb_tri(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2): i64 {
    var sg = _sd_cross_sign(x1 - x0, y1 - y0, x2 - x0, y2 - y0);
    if (sg == 0) { return 0; }
    if (sg < 0) {
        _sd_sb_edge(x0, y0, x1, y1);
        _sd_sb_edge(x1, y1, x2, y2);
        return _sd_sb_edge(x2, y2, x0, y0);
    }
    _sd_sb_edge(x0, y0, x2, y2);
    _sd_sb_edge(x2, y2, x1, y1);
    return _sd_sb_edge(x1, y1, x0, y0);
}

# sd_stroke_disc's 16-gon at (cx, cy), walked backwards: increasing angle is the positive winding.
fn _sd_sb_disc(cx, cy, hw): i64 {
    var fx = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + 15 * 16));
    var fy = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + 15 * 16 + 8));
    var lx = fx;
    var ly = fy;
    var k = 14;
    while (k >= 0) {
        var px = cx + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + k * 16));
        var py = cy + sd_fixed_mul(hw, load64(_sd_sb_trig + k * 16 + 8));
        _sd_sb_edge(lx, ly, px, py);
        lx = px;
        ly = py;
        k = k - 1;
    }
    return _sd_sb_edge(lx, ly, fx, fy);
}

# ---- styled stroking: geometry -----------------------------------
# The perpendicular offset of segment A -> B, magnitude hw: (-dy, dx) * hw / |d|, the direction
# first rescaled by a power of two so its larger component is in [2^20, 2^21). Its rect, its caps
# and both joins call this with the same endpoints, so their shared corners coincide exactly.
# ⚠ Not sd_stroke_seg's `(dy * hw) / isqrt(len2)` on the raw deltas: for a 1-LSB diagonal that
# isqrt is 1, not 1.414, and the join corner lands 41 % of hw outside the band. Rescaled, |d| is
# exact to 2^-20 for any length, and no length overflows (|dy| < 2^21, so hw < 2^42).
# (0, 0) for a zero-length segment.
fn _sd_seg_normal(ax, ay, bx, by, hw): (i64, i64) {
    var dx = bx - ax;
    var dy = by - ay;
    if (dx == 0) {
        if (dy == 0) { return (0, 0); }
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(dx, dy) >= 2097152) {
        dx = sd_asr(dx, 1);
        dy = sd_asr(dy, 1);
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(dx, dy) < 1048576) {
        dx = dx << 1;
        dy = dy << 1;
    }
    var l = sd_isqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    return (((0 - dy) * hw) / l, (dx * hw) / l);
}

# Rescale a direction by a power of two until its larger component is in [2^13, 2^14). Scaling
# does not change an angle; for 16.16 pixel coordinates the down-shifts only drop zero bits.
fn _sd_dir_reduce(dx, dy): (i64, i64) {
    var x = dx;
    var y = dy;
    if (x == 0) {
        if (y == 0) { return (0, 0); }
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(x, y) >= 16384) {
        x = sd_asr(x, 1);
        y = sd_asr(y, 1);
    }
    while (_sd_max_abs(x, y) < 8192) {
        x = x << 1;
        y = y << 1;
    }
    return (x, y);
}

# The join at V between segments P -> V and V -> Q (both non-zero length). `inner` != 0 when V lies
# inside one curve's flattening: the style is replaced by a miter held to 1 + 0.25 px / hw (the
# flatten tolerance over the half-width) that falls back to the round join, not the bevel.
fn _sd_sb_join(px, py, vx, vy, qx, qy, hw, join, ml, inner): i64 {
    var n1x, n1y = _sd_seg_normal(px, py, vx, vy, hw);
    var n2x, n2y = _sd_seg_normal(vx, vy, qx, qy, hw);
    var cr = n1x * n2y - n1y * n2x;     # |n| <= hw, so < hw^2: no overflow
    if (cr == 0) {
        if (n1x * n2x + n1y * n2y >= 0) { return 0; }    # straight on
    }
    var jn = join;
    var lim = ml;
    if (inner != 0) {
        # <= 2^30 + 2^16 (hw >= 1): inside the limit test's L <= 2^31. Unclamped, as it needs no
        # clamp: the tip it admits lies within hw + 0.25 px of V at any hw.
        jn = SD_JOIN_MITER;
        lim = SD_ONE + ((SD_ONE >> 2) << SD_SHIFT) / hw;
    }
    if (jn == SD_JOIN_ROUND) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
    if (cr == 0) {
        # A reversal: the bevel has no area. Inside a curve it is a cusp, which the disc rounds.
        if (inner != 0) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        return 0;
    }
    # cross > 0 turns toward +n, so the offsets diverge on the -n side, and vice versa.
    var sg = 1;
    if (cr > 0) { sg = 0 - 1; }
    var p1x = vx + sg * n1x;
    var p1y = vy + sg * n1y;
    var p2x = vx + sg * n2x;
    var p2y = vy + sg * n2y;
    _sd_sb_tri(vx, vy, p1x, p1y, p2x, p2y);
    if (jn == SD_JOIN_BEVEL) { return 0; }

    # MITER. cos(phi) = D / P for the turn phi = pi - theta; ratio^2 = 1 / cos^2(phi/2) =
    # 2P / (P + D). Past the limit when 2P > L^2 (P + D), i.e. L * u < P * 2^33 with u = L(P + D)
    # (L in 16.16), i.e. u < ceil(P * 2^33 / L) — exact in integers (see the header).
    var e1x, e1y = _sd_dir_reduce(vx - px, vy - py);
    var e2x, e2y = _sd_dir_reduce(qx - vx, qy - vy);
    var s1 = e1x * e1x + e1y * e1y;     # < 2^29
    var s2 = e2x * e2x + e2y * e2y;
    var pp = sd_isqrt(s1 * s2);         # |e1||e2| < 2^29
    var q = pp + e1x * e2x + e1y * e2y; # P + D, below 2^30
    # P >= 2^26 (both reduced components reach 2^13), so the right side is >= 1: a reversal
    # (q <= 0) always bevels here, and q > 0 below.
    if (lim * q < ((pp << 33) + lim - 1) / lim) {
        if (inner != 0) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        return 0;
    }
    # tip = V + s * hw * (u1 + u2) / (1 + cos(phi)), u the unit normals; over the common |e1||e2|
    # that is s * hw * W / (P + D) with W = (-(e1y |e2| + e2y |e1|), e1x |e2| + e2x |e1|).
    var l1 = sd_isqrt(s1);
    var l2 = sd_isqrt(s2);
    var tx = vx + sg * ((hw * (0 - (e1y * l2 + e2y * l1))) / q);
    var ty = vy + sg * ((hw * (e1x * l2 + e2x * l1)) / q);
    return _sd_sb_tri(p1x, p1y, tx, ty, p2x, p2y);
}

# A cap at V for the adjacent segment's offset (ox, oy); `fwd` = 1 at the subpath's end (extend
# along the segment), 0 at its start (extend backwards). e = (oy, -ox) is the direction * hw.
fn _sd_sb_endcap(vx, vy, ox, oy, fwd, cap, hw): i64 {
    if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) { return _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
    if (cap == SD_CAP_SQUARE) {
        if (fwd != 0) { return _sd_sb_rect(vx, vy, vx + oy, vy - ox, ox, oy); }
        return _sd_sb_rect(vx - oy, vy + ox, vx, vy, ox, oy);
    }
    return 0;
}

# Length (16.16) of segment j of an m-vertex subpath (j + 1 wraps to 0).
# ⚠ `run` is 16 B a point, x then y (0.9.0) — see sd_stroke_run.
fn _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, j): i64 {
    var bj = j + 1;
    if (bj == m) { bj = 0; }
    var dx = load64(run + bj * 16) - load64(run + j * 16);
    var dy = load64(run + bj * 16 + 8) - load64(run + j * 16 + 8);
    return sd_isqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
}

# One unit of a de-duplicated subpath: whatever sits at vertex i (the start cap, or the join),
# segment i's rect, and — after the LAST segment — the end cap or the closing join at vertex 0.
# `inner` is the curve-flag array parallel to `run` (1 = the vertex lies inside one curve verb's
# flattening): _sd_sb_inner for a whole path, the dash buffer's own for one dash (src/dash.cyr).
fn _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, i, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var bi = i + 1;
    if (bi == m) { bi = 0; }
    var ax = load64(run + i * 16);
    var ay = load64(run + i * 16 + 8);
    var bx = load64(run + bi * 16);
    var by = load64(run + bi * 16 + 8);
    var ox, oy = _sd_seg_normal(ax, ay, bx, by, hw);
    if (i == 0) {
        if (closed == 0) { _sd_sb_endcap(ax, ay, ox, oy, 0, cap, hw); }
    } else {
        _sd_sb_join(load64(run + (i - 1) * 16), load64(run + (i - 1) * 16 + 8), ax, ay, bx, by,
          hw, join, ml, load8(inner + i));
    }
    _sd_sb_rect(ax, ay, bx, by, ox, oy);
    if (bi == 0) {
        # closed: the vertex-0 join, between this closing segment and segment 0 — a moveto point,
        # so never inside a curve. ⚠ run[1] is at +16 now, not +8 (0.9.0).
        _sd_sb_join(ax, ay, bx, by, load64(run + 16), load64(run + 16 + 8), hw, join, ml, 0);
    } else {
        if (closed == 0) {
            if (bi == m - 1) { _sd_sb_endcap(bx, by, ox, oy, 1, cap, hw); }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# Drop consecutive coincident points of run[0, n) IN PLACE (the write index never passes the read
# index), and — for a closed subpath — the trailing duplicate of point 0, so a zero-length closing
# segment makes no join. `inner` is the parallel curve-flag array, compacted with it: a kept point
# is inside a curve only if every point it absorbs is (where a verb's end meets a coincident curve
# point, the verbs' join style wins). Returns the count kept. ⚠ Every segment of the result has
# non-zero length, which the dash walk (src/dash.cyr) needs as much as the join math does.
fn _sd_sb_dedup(run, inner, n, closed): i64 {
    var m = 0;
    var k = 0;
    while (k < n) {
        var px = load64(run + k * 16);
        var py = load64(run + k * 16 + 8);
        var keep = 1;
        if (m > 0) {
            if (px == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16)) {
                if (py == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16 + 8)) { keep = 0; }
            }
        }
        if (keep != 0) {
            store64(run + m * 16, px);
            store64(run + m * 16 + 8, py);
            store8(inner + m, load8(inner + k));
            m = m + 1;
        } else {
            store8(inner + m - 1, load8(inner + m - 1) & load8(inner + k));
        }
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (m > 1) {
            if (load64(run + 0) == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16)) {
                if (load64(run + 8) == load64(run + (m - 1) * 16 + 8)) { m = m - 1; }
            }
        }
    }
    return m;
}

# Stroke one flattened subpath run (n points, curve flags in `inner`) into the batch.
fn _sd_sb_subpath(cv, run, inner, n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml): i64 {
    var m = _sd_sb_dedup(run, inner, n, closed);

    if (m == 1) {
        if (_sd_sb_room(SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES) == 0) { _sd_sb_flush(cv); }
        var vx = load64(run + 0);
        var vy = load64(run + 8);
        if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) { _sd_sb_disc(vx, vy, hw); }
        if (cap == SD_CAP_SQUARE) { _sd_sb_rect(vx - hw, vy, vx + hw, vy, 0, hw); }
        return 0;
    }

    var s = m - 1;
    if (closed != 0) { s = m; }
    var flushed = 0;
    var i = 0;
    while (i < s) {
        var need = SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES;
        if (closed != 0) {
            if (i == s - 1) { need = SD_STROKE_UNIT_EDGES * 17; }   # + the forward re-emit below
        }
        if (_sd_sb_room(need) == 0) {
            _sd_sb_flush(cv);
            if (i > 0) { flushed = 1; }
            # Re-emit the stretch before unit i so its pieces meet unit i inside one fill.
            var j = i;
            var back = 0;
            var going = 1;
            while (going != 0) {
                going = 0;
                if (j > 0) {
                    if (i - j < 16) {
                        if (back < 2 * hw + 3 * SD_ONE) {
                            j = j - 1;
                            back = back + _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, j);
                            going = 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
            while (j < i) {
                _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, j, hw, cap, join, ml);
                j = j + 1;
            }
        }
        _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, i, hw, cap, join, ml);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (closed != 0) {
        if (flushed != 0) {
            # The vertex-0 join just went into a batch that may not hold segment 0's side of it.
            var f = 0;
            var ahead = 0;
            while (f < 16) {
                if (ahead < 2 * hw + 3 * SD_ONE) {
                    if (f < s - 1) {
                        _sd_sb_unit(run, inner, m, closed, f, hw, cap, join, ml);
                        ahead = ahead + _sd_sb_seglen(run, m, f);
                    }
                }
                f = f + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

# (Re)open a subpath at (cx, cy) when a drawing verb follows a CLOSE: the run restarts at the
# moveto point, as the fill restarts its edges there.
# ⚠ `has` REPLACES THE OLD `cur != 0` TEST (0.9.0): the current point is a coordinate pair now and
# (0, 0) is the origin, so the caller passes its own flag — a subpath restarting at the origin used
# to be dropped by a pointer test and no longer can be.
fn _sd_sb_restart(run, run_n, cx, cy, has): i64 {
    if (run_n == 0) {
        if (has != 0) {
            store64(run + 0, cx);
            store64(run + 8, cy);
            return 1;
        }
    }
    return run_n;
}

# Flag run[n0, n) as one curve verb's flattening: every point is inside the curve but the last,
# the verb's own end point, where the next verb's join style applies.
fn _sd_sb_mark_curve(n0, n): i64 {
    var k = n0;
    while (k < n) {
        store8(_sd_sb_inner + k, 1);
        k = k + 1;
    }
    if (n > n0) { store8(_sd_sb_inner + n - 1, 0); }
    return 0;
}

# One subpath of the run, to the batch — whole (`dashed` == 0) or cut into dashes (src/dash.cyr).
# ⚠ The ONE call that runs from this file into dash.cyr, and the reason the [lib] order reads
# stroke.cyr then dash.cyr: dash.cyr is built ON the stroker (it cuts a run into pieces and hands
# each back to _sd_sb_subpath), and this is the hook that lets both entry points share ONE verb
# walk, so a flatten fix cannot reach solid strokes and miss dashed ones.
fn _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed): i64 {
    if (dashed != 0) {
        return _sd_dash_subpath(cv, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_sb_inner, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml);
    }
    return _sd_sb_subpath(cv, _sd_stroke_run, _sd_sb_inner, run_n, closed, hw, cap, join, ml);
}

# The styled stroker's verb walk: flatten `path`'s curve verbs at tol = SD_ONE >> 2 into the
# stroker's run (raster.cyr's scratch) with the parallel curve flags, hand each subpath to
# _sd_sb_dispatch, and fill the batch once at the end. Both public styled entry points are this
# walk plus their own argument checks; the caller has already run _sd_scratch_init / _sd_sb_init
# and cleared _sd_run_trunc.
# ⚠ The run and its curve flags GROW (0.7.0): read _sd_stroke_run at each use, never a copy taken
# before a push.
#
# Returns SADISH_OK, or SADISH_ERR_OOM if anything this call reached was refused (0.7.2): a curve's
# flatten mid-points, a run point or curve flag the growth could not take, an edge the batch could
# not take, a dash piece the buffer could not take, the flush's accumulator. ⛔ THE WALK RUNS TO THE
# END EITHER WAY and the batch is still flushed — the report is collected at the bottom, not thrown
# at the first refusal, so a stroke that loses one curve's mid-points still draws every other piece
# it can. What is on the canvas afterwards is that prefix (the header's no-rollback rule).
fn _sd_styled_walk(cv, path, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed): i64 {
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    var tol = SD_ONE >> 2;
    var run_n = 0;
    var fbuf = _sd_stroke_fbuf;
    var fctx = _sd_stroke_fctx;
    _sd_stroke_oom = 0;
    # The flatten's truncation verdict, scoped to this call as sd_fill_impl scopes it; the caller's
    # standing verdict is put back at the bottom. (sd_canvas_stroke_path does the same.)
    var flat_was = _sd_flat_trunc;
    _sd_flat_trunc = 0;

    # ⛔ COORDINATES AND `has_cur` (0.9.0) — src/raster.cyr's fill walk, applied to the run: the
    # current point is a pair of numbers, so 0 is the origin and the "no moveto yet" test is a flag.
    var verbs = load64(path + SD_PATH_VERBS_OFFSET);
    var curx = 0;
    var cury = 0;
    var has_cur = 0;
    var startx = 0;
    var starty = 0;
    var vi = 0;
    var pi = 0;
    while (vi < n_verbs) {
        var verb = load64(verbs + vi * 8);
        if (verb == SD_VERB_MOVETO) {
            if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 0, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
            curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
            cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
            has_cur = 1;
            startx = curx;
            starty = cury;
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + 0, curx);
            store64(_sd_stroke_run + 8, cury);
            run_n = 1;
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_LINETO) {
            if (has_cur != 0) {         # a drawing verb before any moveto is skipped
                run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
                curx = sd_path_point_x(path, pi);
                cury = sd_path_point_y(path, pi);
                run_n = _sd_run_push(run_n, curx, cury, 1);
            }
            pi = pi + 1;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_QUADTO) {
            if (has_cur == 0) { pi = pi + 2; vi = vi + 1; continue; }
            run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
            var qex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1);
            var qey = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1);
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
            _sd_flatten_quad_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi), qex, qey, 0, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
            var qn0 = run_n;
            run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 1);
            _sd_sb_mark_curve(qn0, run_n);
            curx = qex;
            cury = qey;
            pi = pi + 2;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CUBICTO) {
            if (has_cur == 0) { pi = pi + 3; vi = vi + 1; continue; }
            run_n = _sd_sb_restart(_sd_stroke_run, run_n, curx, cury, has_cur);
            var kex = sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 2);
            var key = sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 2);
            store64(fctx + 0, fbuf);
            store64(fctx + 8, 0);
            store64(fctx + 16, SD_FLATTEN_CAP);
            _sd_flatten_cubic_xy(fctx, curx, cury, sd_path_point_x(path, pi),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi), sd_path_point_x(path, pi + 1),
              sd_path_point_y(path, pi + 1), kex, key, 0, tol, SD_FLATTEN_MAX_DEPTH);
            var cn0 = run_n;
            run_n = _sd_run_append(run_n, fctx, 1);
            _sd_sb_mark_curve(cn0, run_n);
            curx = kex;
            cury = key;
            pi = pi + 3;
        }
        if (verb == SD_VERB_CLOSE) {
            if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 1, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
            run_n = 0;
            curx = startx;
            cury = starty;
        }
        vi = vi + 1;
    }
    if (run_n > 0) { _sd_sb_dispatch(cv, run_n, 0, hw, cap, join, ml, dashed); }
    # ⚠ The final flush's own code is not read here: it SETS _sd_stroke_oom, which is also the only
    # way the mid-walk flushes (_sd_sb_subpath's, four frames down) can report anything at all.
    _sd_sb_flush(cv);

    var rc = SADISH_OK;
    if (_sd_flat_trunc != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (flat_was != 0) { _sd_flat_trunc = 1; }
    if (_sd_run_trunc != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    if (_sd_stroke_oom != 0) { rc = SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    return rc;
}

# ================================================================
# sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex — stroke `path` at `width` (16.16 full width) with cap style `cap`
# (SD_CAP_BUTT | SD_CAP_ROUND | SD_CAP_SQUARE), join style `join` (SD_JOIN_MITER |
# SD_JOIN_ROUND | SD_JOIN_BEVEL) and `miter_limit` (16.16 ratio, SVG stroke-miterlimit; SVG's
# default is 4.0 = 4 * SD_ONE; used only by SD_JOIN_MITER), MAX-combined into `cv`'s coverage.
#
# Returns SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS for cv == 0 or an unknown cap/join (nothing drawn),
# SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH for a 0 or verb-less path, SADISH_ERR_OOM when an allocation this stroke
# reached was refused (the header's refusal block — the canvas keeps the prefix that was drawn),
# SADISH_OK otherwise — width <= 0 draws nothing.
# ⚠ SD_CAP_ROUND + SD_JOIN_ROUND IS sd_canvas_stroke_path, exactly — and so, alone among the nine
# pairs, keeps its 0.4.0 geometry: a drawing verb after CLOSE continues the closed run instead of
# starting at the moveto point, and pieces meet by per-pass MAX (a pixel two of them split reads
# the larger part, not the sum). Semantics + bounds: the header of this file.
# ================================================================
# @public
fn sd_canvas_stroke_path_ex(cv, path, width, cap, join, miter_limit): i64 {
    if (cv == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (path == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    var n_verbs = sd_path_verb_count(path);
    if (n_verbs == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_EMPTY_PATH; }
    if (cap < SD_CAP_BUTT) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (cap > SD_CAP_SQUARE) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join < SD_JOIN_MITER) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (join > SD_JOIN_BEVEL) { return SADISH_ERR_BOUNDS; }
    if (cap == SD_CAP_ROUND) {
        if (join == SD_JOIN_ROUND) { return sd_canvas_stroke_path(cv, path, width); }
    }
    var hw = sd_asr(width, 1);
    if (hw <= 0) { return SADISH_OK; }
    var ml = miter_limit;
    if (ml < SD_ONE) { ml = SD_ONE; }
    if (ml > SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX) { ml = SD_MITER_LIMIT_MAX; }

    # The stroker's run/fbuf/fctx (raster.cyr) stay live across the batch flushes; the batch is
    # this file's own scratch. ⛔ Each guard is on the BLOCK the walk stores through, not on the
    # initialiser's status — sd_fill_impl's reasoning; both publishes are all-or-nothing.
    _sd_scratch_init();
    if (_sd_stroke_run == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_sb_init();                      # the batch is empty: every call ends in _sd_sb_flush
    if (_sd_sb_edges == 0) { return SADISH_ERR_OOM; }
    _sd_run_trunc = 0;
    return _sd_styled_walk(cv, path, hw, cap, join, ml, 0);
}
